## 1. Introduction
Ion-selective membranes (ISMs) have a wide variety of applications in batteries, water purification systems, natural resource extraction (e.g., lithium ions),[1,2] microfluidic systems,[3] reverse electrodialysis,[4] energy conversion,[5] biosensors,[6] potentiometric sensors for detecting metal ions, small molecules, DNA, microRNA, enzymes, and proteins[7] and many other areas. However, one major issue that has yet to be solved by ISMs is to realize high monovalent ion selectivity. Much research efforts have been devoted to improving ion selectivity of various membranes such as Graphene Oxide (GO),[8,9] Vermiculite-based membranes,[10] MXenes,[11] and post-transition metal/polymer composites.[12] Among 2D membranes, GO membranes in particular have been investigated thoroughly, for instance, a variety of guest molecules have been introduced in between the GO nanosheets to change either nanochannels’ size or their surface charge.[9,13] Nevertheless, none of these membranes shows a selectivity ratio of lithium to other monovalent ions higher than 10. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) based membranes have shown great promise to achieve ionic separation. For instance, Gue et  al. incorporated PSS molecule into HKUST to produce a MOF membrane with a high mono­valent $\mathrm{Li^{+}/K^{+}}$ selectivity ratio of 99 being reported.[14] This shows the potential of MOFs or PSS molecules in separating ${\mathrm{Li^{+}}}$ ions from other monovalent ions. However, the tested membranes were placed parallel with the flow direction, that is, transport was lateral to the surface of the membrane. This configuration reduces the membranes’ ion conductance significantly and limits its application in industry. In this configuration, the surface area of the membrane exposed to the electrolyte is minimized which in turn minimizes the total ionic flow through the membrane. Besides, what gives a high selectivity to these types of membranes is the length of the membrane, and reducing its length or changing the orientation of the membrane would not yield the same high selectivity ratios. As a result, a new strategy in the design of membranes is required to produce a highly monovalent ISM.
Nature offers great inspirations to overcome this bottleneck. Years of evolution have yielded biological ion channels that are generally asymmetric in their morphology, structure, surface charge, or functional groups. This versatility has resulted in channels with $\mathrm{K^{+}/N a^{+}}$ selectivity reaching as high as $10^{4}.$ .[15] This high selectivity stems from their conical shape, with their narrow side being around only $12\mathrm{~\AA~}$ .[16] Asymmetric nanochannels, especially conical nanochannels, have been fabricated before, but these artificially made channels usually have dimensions larger than $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ . These channels show a diode-like behavior in which the current passing through them is significantly dependent on the direction of the applied voltage. This phenomenon is an indication of the preferred direction for ion current[17] and is known as ionic current rectification (ICR). These observations have been confirmed by theoretical simulations as well.[18]
MOFs have the advantage of being highly tunable and as a result, they have been produced with a variety of structures and properties.[19] Lu et  al. fabricated an asymmetric MOF-based membrane with PET and achieved high monovalent to divalent ion selectivity in addition to observing high ICR,[20] however their membrane is made of bullet-shaped PET channels ( $500\mathrm{nm}$ in size) which are filled with MOF particles and as a result could not show high monovalent ion selectivity. In view of this, we propose using MOFs to construct a membrane with asymmetrical nanochannels at angstrom scale. MOF on MOF core-shell structures have been synthesized in previous studies, but have yet to be translated to separation membranes.[21] Asymmetrical pores can be achieved by depositing a MOF thin film on another MOF layer. Zhu et  al.[22] previously prepared a $\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}$ -tpt-on- $\mathrm{{Cu(I)}}$ -tpt membrane for the purpose of adsorption and fluorescence sensing of $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ , but the structure of the nanochannels was not the main interest and was not explored for ion separation. Asymmetrical pores can be achieved by depositing a MOF thin film on another MOF layer. At the atomic scale interface between these two layers, the nanopores’ shape could potentially transform. The formed membrane mimics step changes in pore size and cavities in nature which can provide enhanced selectivity for monovalent ions.
Three MOF-on-MOF membranes were constructed in this study to investigate the effect of asymmetry in morphology and the surface charge on nanochannels’ ion transport. One of the membranes was formed by depositing ZIF-L on a ZIF-8 MOF layer (named ZIF-8/ZIF-L membrane), while the others were prepared by depositing a ZIF-8 thin film on a UiO$66\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cdot}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{N}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ MOF layer with the help of polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP)
molecules as structure-directing agents (named $\mathrm{UiO-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\$ ZIF-8(PVP) membrane). ZIF-8/ZIF-L’s nanopores are relatively similar in size compared to $\mathrm{UiO{-}66{-}N H_{2}/Z I F{-}8(P V P)}$ , which provides a benchmark for investigating the effect of pore size difference on membranes’ ion selectivity. It has been shown that nanochannels’ surface charge asymmetry enhances the ICR effect caused by morphological asymmetry.[17] Therefore, we prepared a third MOF-on-MOF membrane by incorporating poly(sodium-4-styrenesulfonate) (PSS) molecule into the UiO- $66\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}}$ MOF thin film and then depositing a ZIF-8 layer on top of it with the help of PVP as done in the previous step (named UiO- $66{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH_{2}}(\mathrm{PSS})/\mathrm{ZIF{\mathrm{-}}8(\mathrm{PVP})}$ membrane). The inclusion of the PSS molecule introduces a negative surface charge into the first layer, resulting in surface charge asymmetry throughout the nanochannel.
It was found that membranes with both morphological and surface charge asymmetry show the highest monovalent Li-selectivity $(\mathrm{K^{+}}/\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ of 84) coupled with an ICR ratio of as high as 105 and ionic flux of $\mathrm{1.14\Li^{+}\ m o l\ m^{-2}\ h^{-1}}$ . The high rectification ratio of the membrane gives it potential applications in other areas as well such as gas separation, energy conversion, biosensors, and water purification.[23] The fundamental methods developed in this paper can be used to produce other membranes with asymmetrical nanochannels with multiple energy barriers. The fabrication methods used in this study are quite straightforward, making it an ideal candidate for adaptation to produce other membranes for alternative applications.
## 2. MOF-on-MOF Membranes
In this paper, we set out to investigate the effect of various asymmetries (nanochannels’ shape, size, and surface charge) on ion transport behavior of the membrane. This concept is implemented using the most widely available MOFs (namely ZIF MOFs and UiO-66 MOFs). These two MOFs have been commercialized and as a result, have a simple and straightforward fabrication procedure. By using these two MOFs the synthesis procedure of the MOF-on-MOF membrane is facilitated. Besides, the ZIF-8 and ZIF-L have nanochannels similar in size but different in shape, which makes the ZIF-8/ZIF-L membrane asymmetric in terms of its nanochannels’ shape. ZIF-8 and $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ s nanochannels’ size is different (ratio of almost 2:1) which makes the UiO-66-NH2/ZIF-8(PVP) membrane asymmetric in terms of its nanochannels size and shape. Using $\mathrm{UiO{-}66{-}N H_{2}/Z I F{-}8(P V P)}$ the effect of morphological asymmetry on ion transport can be investigated. In addition, the purpose of adding PSS to make $\mathrm{UiO{-}66{-}N H_{2}(P S S)/Z I F}.$ 8(PVP) is to investigate the effect of asymmetry in surface charge distribution as well. This membrane is both asymmetric in its morphology and in its surface chemistry, since PSS molecules are only located throughout the UiO- $66\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cdot}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{N}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ part and not the whole membrane. As a result, its nanochannels have a nonuniform distribution of surface charge.
membrane, an additional asymmetry has been introduced by including PSS molecules to act as nucleophile traps. Nucleophile molecules such as PSS have a high affinity to bond with positively charged atomic nuclei (cations used in this study). PSS’s sulfonate groups have a high affinity towards ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ ions.[2,14]
## 3. Characterization
This conceptual configuration serves as an ideal model to be adopted for highly selective ionic separation membranes. Our asymmetrical fused MOF membranes were made through a sequential layer-by-layer MOF deposition process onto a polydopamine/polyethyleneimine (PDA/PEI) functionalized anodic aluminum oxide (AAO) substrate (Figures S3–S5, Supporting Information, and Figure  2a). For the UiO-66-NH2(PSS)/ZIF8(PVP) membrane, PSS was also intercalated into the first layer $(\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ by in-situ encapsulation. In addition, PVP was used as a cross-linking agent to connect the separated MOF layers (Figure  2a).[24–26] Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) characterization of the synthesized $\mathrm{UiO{-}66{\mathrm{-}}N H_{2}(P S S)/Z I F{\mathrm{-}}}$ 8(PVP) membrane showed that the deposited MOF layers are continuous without major pinholes. Relatively larger crystals of ZIF-8 are successfully deposited on $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (Figure 2a). Energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy in scanning transmission electron microscopy (STEM-EDS) mapping on the membrane cross-section indicates the formation of two distinct componential layers, with good agreement with the atomic density contrast shown in the bright field (BF) image. The distinct interface between two layers can be readily observed in the EDS analysis (Figure  2c and Figure S6, Supporting Information), where the $\mathrm{zr}$ and S elements from the UiO-66- $\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cdotNH}}_{2}(\ensuremath{\mathrm{PSS}})$ layer as well as $Z\mathrm{n}$ element from the ZIF-8(PVP) layer are identified (Figure 2d). It should be noted that the EDS spectrum in Figure  2d covers the whole field of view in Figure  2b. Therefore, other elements such as Pt on the film’s top surface, which served as a protective coating for FIB, and Al from the AAO membrane substrate were also detected. Furthermore, the striations seen in the $\mathrm{UiO{-}66{\mathrm{-}}N H_{2}(P S S)/Z I F{\mathrm{-}}8(P V P)}$ film in Figure 2B,2C are due to artifacts from FIB in the preparation of the cross-section sample for TEM.[27]
The membrane constructed by this process consists of a $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}(\mathrm{PSS})$ layer with thickness estimated to be $125~\pm~15~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ and a ZIF-8(PVP) layer with a thickness of $571\pm18~\mathrm{nm}$ , collectively forming a thin structure that resembles bio-inspired asymmetrical gateways. As mentioned previously, two other membranes with asymmetrical nanochannels were also prepared as benchmarks. UiO-66-NH2/ZIF-8(PVP) was produced to compare its performance to UiO ${\cdot66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}}(\mathrm{PSS})/$ ZIF-8(PVP), which utilizes the nucleophilic entrapment effect of PSS. On the other hand, ZIF-8/ZIF-L is constructed by fusing ZIF-L and ZIF-8 using a similar architecture to take advantage of the unique cavity shape of ZIF-L (Figure S7, Supporting Information).[28,29] Successful fabrication of ZIF-8/ZIF-L membrane is then verified through cross-section SEM, which showed a layer of leaf-like structures (ZIF-L) on top of ZIF-8 film (Figure S8, Supporting Information).
The crystalline structure of the MOF membrane was studied using X-ray diffraction (XRD) (Figure 2g). The XRD pattern of ZIF-8(PVP) film on PDA/PEI coated AAO membrane exhibits distinct peaks at $7.4^{\circ}$ , $10.4^{\circ}$ , $12.7^{\circ}$ , $14.7^{\circ}$ , $16.5^{\circ}$ , $18.1^{\circ}$ , $22.2^{\circ}$ , $24.5^{\circ}$ , and $26.7^{\circ}$ which is in good agreement with the simulated ZIF-8 pattern from the database[29,30] and previous reports.[31,32] The feature peaks of UiO ${\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}(\mathrm{PSS})$ can also be identified at $2\theta$ positions of $7.3^{\circ}$ and $8.4^{\circ}$ . Furthermore, the peaks corresponding to the ZIF-8(PVP) phase and UiO-6 ${\mathsf{G}}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}{\big(}{\mathrm{PSS}}{\big)}$ phase can be identified in the $\mathrm{UiO{-}66{-}N H_{2}(P S S)/Z I F{-}8(P V)}$ P) membrane. There are no peaks for the AAO membrane substrate.
FT-IR spectroscopy was used to examine the surface chemistry of the asymmetrical MOF membrane and its substrate (Figure 2e). The formation of ZIF-8 in ZIF-8/ZIF-L and UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH_{2}(P S S)/Z I F{\cdot}}8(\mathrm{PVP})$ membranes are identified from characteristic $\mathrm{{zn-N}}$ bonding at $421\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and in-plane bending of imidazole ring at $1150~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in the low-frequency region (Figure 2e and Figure S9a, Supporting Information).[31,32] On the other hand, the formation of the UiO-6 $\mathsf{j}6{\cdot}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}(\mathrm{PSS})$ layer is observed through the presence of $\mathsf{Z r}{-}\mathsf{O}$ stretching at $768~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ [32,33] However, UiO-66-NH2(PSS)/ZIF-8(PVP)’s $z\mathrm{r}{-}0$ peak could not be easily observed due to limitations in the penetration depth and attenuation of the IR beam in the film, coupled with the relatively small absorbance peak for $z\mathrm{r}{-}0$ . Nonetheless, the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ functional group from the ligand can be observed with a strong and broad peak spanning from 2600 to $3600{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ (Figure 2E and Figure S9c, Supporting Information). The presence of PSS in the composite was also evident by peaks at 1008, 1036, 1125, and $1188~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ from pure PSS molecule associated with ${\mathrm{R}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{SO}}_{3}$ stretching (Figure  2e and Figure S9b, Supporting Information).[34] MD simulations demonstrate that the PSS is concentrated near the MOF’s cavity entries (Figure S10, Supporting Information). To further study the effect of polymeric guest molecules on the MOF (PVP in ZIF-8 and PSS in $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{\cdot}$ , the as-synthesized particles formed in the bulk solution were collected for zeta potential analysis. The addition of PVP during the synthesis of ZIF-8 has shifted the zeta potential of ZIF-8 from $17.07\pm0.95$ to $-4.59\pm0.39\mathrm{\mV}.$ Similarly, the zeta potential of $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ particles with PSS addition decreased from $28.17\pm1.12\$ to $19.07\pm1.11\mathrm{mV}.$ In both cases, the introduction of negative charged PVP and PSS molecules has shifted the surface zeta potential of MOF particles to be more negative (Figure 2f and Table S4, Supporting Information).
While zeta potential measurement can show the effect of adding polymeric guest molecules on the surface charge of MOF, it cannot reveal the interaction between the guest mole­cule and MOF. In particular, understanding the role of PVP requires further characterization. PVP molecules have been used as structure-directing agents in many studies.[35] Usually, PVP controls the growth and structure of nanoparticles and is not present in the final product. We did extensive characterizations to find the role of PVP and determine whether it remains in the MOF film post-synthesis. Since UV–vis spectroscopy could not detect PVP in the remaining solution after synthesis due to the presence of 2-methylimidazole as an interfering compound (Figure S11, Supporting Information), we used FT-IR to look for characteristic peaks of PVP in the membrane. Figure 2h illustrates the FT-IR spectra of the PVP mole­ cule and three types of MOF membranes. The range between $1600{-}1700\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ is attributed to $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ stretching, which can be found in PVP but cannot be observed in any of the membranes. The FT-IR of the UiO $){-66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}(\mathrm{PSS})}/{\mathrm{ZIF}{\mathrm{-}}8(\mathrm{PVP})}$ and UiO- $66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}(\mathrm{PSS})/\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ (without the use of PVP) are similar, which suggests that the PVP molecule is absent in the MOF film. However, this might be simply because the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ bond signal of PVP was too weak to be picked up by FTIR. Therefore, XPS was employed to further probe the chemical interaction between PVP and MOF (Figures S12–S14, Supporting Information). Subsurface information in the film was also revealed through depth-profiling XPS, which combines ion gun etch cycles with XPS analysis. Figure S15, Supporting Information, shows the C1s spectra of PVP, ZIF-8, ZIF-8(PVP), and UiO-66-NH2(PSS)/ZIF-8(PVP). Deconvolution of the XPS spectra of ZIF-8(PVP) and UiO-66-NH2(PSS)/ZIF-8(PVP) reveals a peak at $287.0\ \mathrm{~eV},$ which corresponds to the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ functional group of PVP.[36] This peak is not present in the
ZIF-8 membrane without the addition of PVP. After etching the UiO-66-NH2(PSS)/ZIF-8(PVP) film to a depth of approximately $400~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ , the deconvoluted XPS spectrum revealed a peak centered at $286.2\ \mathrm{eV},$ which is assigned to the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C-O}}$ functional group.[37] This indicates an interaction between the polar carbonyl group of PVP and MOF, potentially forming a coordination bond with the metallic ions of MOF. During the synthesis of ZIF-8 on UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}(\mathrm{PSS})$ , the presence of PVP at the interface can help to facilitate the growth of ZIF-8 onto UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}(\mathrm{PSS})$ by coordination. This is in agreement with the observation by Gu et  al.,[25] where the growth of MIL${125.}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ onto $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was possible due to the presence of PVP. In addition to XPS, Synchrotron Thz-FIR characterizations were also conducted on the membrane. However, they could not reveal anything since the peaks in the usable range were too small to obtain any valuable information.
## 4. Ion Transport through the Membranes
To study the ion transport properties of the MOF-on-MOF membranes, current-voltage (I-V) curves of all membranes were recorded in $0.01\textbf{M}$ LiCl solutions (Figure  3a–c). Asymmetric I-V curves are an indication of the preferential direction of the membranes and their ionic rectification effect. The rectification arises from different pore diameters of fused membranes, here $6\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ for UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ and $3.4\mathring\mathrm{A}$ for ZIF-8.[29,38] It should be noted that the incorporation of polymeric guest molecules (PSS and PVP) may change the pore sizes of the MOFs, but the change is not expected to be too significant based on previous studies. For example, the incorporation of PSS into HKUST slightly decreased the pore size from 0.67 to $0.62\ \mathrm{nm},^{[14]}$ while there was no noticeable change in pore size with the addition PVP into ZIF-8.[24] The ionic current is expected to be higher when the cations flow from the smaller (i.e., higher charge density) to the larger end of the nanochannel (i.e., lower charge density).[20] Therefore, given the asymmetric structure of the MOF-on-MOF membranes, the direction of the applied external electric field, as the main driving force of ion migration, would affect the ionic conduction across the membrane. Herein, we used linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) to record the current-voltage curves.
The voltage is swept from $^{-1}$ to $+1$ volts in each measurement and therefore the direction of the applied electric field changes in each recording. As a result, all the membranes were tested with both directions of the applied electric field. However, we also changed the position of electrodes with regard to the membrane (named reversed electrode position in contrast with the normal electrode position) to confirm the diode-like behavior of the membrane. Negative voltages (i.e., when the applied potential is swept from $^{-1}$ to 0 volts) for the state of normal electrode position (blue line) equates to the ions moving from smaller to larger cavities, while positive voltage (i.e., when the applied potential is swept from 0 to 1 volt) equates to ions moving from larger to smaller cavities (e.g., from $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ to ZIF-8). The reverse is true for the reversed electrodes (red line). As shown in Figure 3a–c, the slope of red line at positive voltages (right side of graph) is very close to that of blue line at negative voltages (left side of graph) and vice versa. In other words, the asymmetrical architecture creates a gate that mainly permits ions to go from the smaller to the larger cavity and not in the reversed direction no matter how the electrodes are positioned relative to the membrane. Since reversing the position of electrodes does not change the behavior of the membrane and only reverses the slope of the I-V curves, all the future experiments were conducted with the electrodes being in their normal positioning. ICR ratio defined as the ratio of the higher slope of the I-V curves to their lower slope, is plotted in Figure 3d for all the membranes. The MOF-on-MOF membranes all exhibit ICR due to the asymmetrical nature of their nanochannels. However, increasing asymmetry either in structure or in the chemistry of the nanochannels enhances the ICR effect. For instance, UiO-66-NH $_2/$ ZIF-8(PVP) has a higher ICR than ZIF-8/ZIF-L because of its higher asymmetrical structure. But more importantly, the presence of PSS in UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH_{2}(P S S)/Z I F{\cdot}}8(\mathrm{PVP})$ has significantly increased its ICR ratio. It has previously been observed that apart from improving ion conductivity, PSS can also serve as a nucleophile trap with a varying affinity for different alkali and alkaline earth metal ions which may contribute to the enhanced ICR.[2,14] It is envisioned that other conducting polymers such as PEDOT (6) and poly­ pyrrole (7, 8) can also be incorporated as guest molecules into $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , where the effect on ICR can be tested in future studies. UiO- $66{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH}_{2}(\mathrm{PSS})/\mathrm{ZIF}{\mathrm{-}}8(\mathrm{PVP})$ shows a remarkable ICR ratio of 105 for $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ which is promising and adds to the potential applications of this membrane in other areas. It should be noted that this ICR behavior was not observed for the case of membranes with single MOF films of UiO-6 ${\mathrm{6-NH}}_{2}(\mathrm{PSS})$ and ZIF-8(PVP), as shown in Figures S23 and S24, Supporting Information, respectively.
To measure each membrane’s selectivity, the I-V curves of five other electrolytes with the same ionic strength $(0.01\textbf{\textup{M}})$ were recorded for all three MOF-on-MOF membranes (Figures S16–S18, Supporting Information). All the membranes show the ICR for all electrolytes; however, the rectification ratio and the current for each ion are different. Considering the smaller passable cavity of the ZIF-8/ZIF-L channel $(\approx3.4\mathrm{\AA})$ , the expected lower current is accompanied by a lower degree of rectification due to the smaller difference in pore size between the two MOFs (Figure 3a and Figure S19a, Supporting Information). The substitution of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ for the ZIF-L drastically increases the current (up to ten times) while also increasing the channel’s rectification ratio. While the larger size of $\mathrm{UiO-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ is responsible for the enhanced flow rate, the more significant energy barrier caused by the larger difference between the passable cavities of the two MOFs suppresses the flow of cations in the opposite direction, consequently increasing the rectification ratio (Figure  3b and Figure S19b, Supporting Information). For the MOF-on-MOF membrane with incorporated polymers, UiO- $66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}(\mathrm{PSS})/\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8(\mathrm{PVP})$ , the ionic flow rate has an additional $50\%$ rise, with the highest rectification efficiency (Figure  3c and Figure S19c, Supporting Information). While the hierarchical structure of the ion transport channels is expected to be largely comparable, the inclusion of the additional nucleophile PSS molecules drastically changes the nature of the pores. In addition, UiO- $66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}(\mathrm{PSS})/$ ZIF-8(PVP) channels have a major improvement over the rectification compared to $\mathrm{UiO{-}66{-}N H_{2}/Z I F{-}8(P V P)}$ or ZIF-8/ZIF-L. Again, the synergy between the shrinking accessible cavity and PSS can significantly increase rectification compared to the approach of solely reducing the passable cavity’s size (Figure S20, Supporting Information). It should be noted that, even though all asymmetrical designs depicted a degree of rectification, $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ cations experience the highest rectification effect amongst the tested cations.
Since all the membranes’ I-V curves are non-linear and the current under positive and negative voltages is different, the membrane’s selectivity ratio for positive voltages and negative voltages is also different. Therefore, for each membrane, we can obtain two distinct selectivity ratios. The selectivity ratios depend on the direction of flow and current. If the higher currents (at negative voltages) are divided, the selectivity ratios would be different from if the lower currents (positive voltages) are divided on each other. Therefore, two selectivity ratios are introduced here:
The selectivity ratios are plotted in Figure  3d. Negative voltage selectivity rations represent the selectivity when the ions move from smaller to larger cavities, while positive voltage selectivity ratios are related to when the ions move from larger to smaller cavities. As can be seen, the positive voltage selectivity ratios are higher than negative voltage (when ionic current is higher) selectivity ratios. This means that when the ions move against the nanochannels’ preferred direction, the membranes’ selectivity ratio is higher. In this case, the lithium ions conductance through the membrane is much lower than other monovalent ions. Selectivity evaluation of AAO and AAO/PDA substrates show little or non-selective performance, with a slight preference towards transporting $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ (Figures S21 and S22, Supporting Information). This is also the case for one-layer UiO- $66\mathrm{-N}\mathrm{H}_{2}(\mathrm{PSS})$ and ZIF-8(PVP) membranes (Figures S23 and S24, Supporting Information). However, MOF-on-MOF membranes have a much higher selectivity ratio, especially their positive voltage selectivity, largely due to their asymmetric nanochannels (Figure  3d). For instance, the ZIF-8/ZIF-L membrane has a $\mathrm{K^{+}/L i^{+}}$ selectivity ratio of 3.5, which is higher than single-layer MOF membranes. But ZIF-8 and ZIF-L pores are relatively similar in size, and as a result, the selectivity enhancement is not significant. $\mathrm{UiO-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\$ ZIF-8(PVP)’s selectivity ratios, on the other hand, are much higher than ZIF-8/ZIF-L because of the higher difference in pore diameters of its constituent MOF membranes (6 to 3.4Å). However, $\mathrm{UiO{-}66{\mathrm{-}}N H_{2}(P S S)/Z I F{\mathrm{-}}8(P V P)}$ membrane has the highest $\mathrm{K^{+}/L i^{+}}$ selectivity ratio (as high as 84) among all other MOF-on-MOF membranes (Table S5, Supporting Information, and Figure 3d). This is due to nucleophile PSS molecules, which add charge asymmetry throughout the nanochannel and have a high affinity towards $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ ions.
## 5. Mechanism of Ion Selectivity in MOF-on-MOF Membranes
Quantum mechanics (QM)/molecular mechanics methods and molecular dynamics (MD) simulations were employed to analyze the mechanism of ion selectivity in the MOF-on-MOF membranes. Our simulations showed that the abrupt change in the size of the nanochannels due to their asymmetrical structure disturb the cations’ hydration shell. The size difference between the fused passable cavities creates an energy barrier designated the “energy surge wall”. This mechanism is based on the sudden change in the size of the cavity, thus creating a spatial barrier that reduces the number of water molecules in the cation’s hydration shell. The energy surge wall that emerged in $\mathrm{UiO-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\$ ZIF-8(PVP) is the reason for its higher selectivity compared to ZIF-8/ZIF-L. However, in the case of UiO- $\mathsf{36{-}N H_{2}}(\mathsf{P S S})/$ ZIF-8(PVP), introducing nucleophile species into the structure of the MOF has changed the electron density composition of these cavities and altered the entrapment properties of the passable cavity, thus leading to the emergence of a new mechanism, named here as energy surge baffle (more details are presented in Figure S25, Supporting Information). This mechanism is based on the sudden change in the cavity size in the presence of nucleophile species. The result is a two-step process: i) confrontation of cations with the sudden change in the cavity size perturbs their hydration shells, ii) with their hydration shells perturbed, these ions become more susceptible to the nucleophile species intercalated inside the MOFs and become trapped (Figures S1, S2, and S29, Supporting Information). This combined approach (nucleophile traps and spatial hindrance) provides a highly tunable energy barrier for different cations. The same mechanism is utilized here to selectively trap $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ ions since it possesses the lowest number of water molecules in its first hydration layer (compared to the other mono- and divalent cations), resulting in a selectivity ratio (against $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{Na^{+}})$ as high as 80. Figure  4a explains the reason for the high selectivity of this mechanism. At the upper section, the energy surge wall mechanism is demonstrated through the energy barrier that ${\mathrm{Li^{+}}}$ cations experience at different sections of the channel. Herein, the energy barrier in the path of the ions is shown as they move from the larger cavities to the smaller cavities (e.g., from UiO-66 to ZIF-8). This energy barrier’s growth is relatively gradual when the cation passes in between MOF sections and slightly favors the entrapment of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ cations at the transitional zone between the $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and ZIF-8(PVP) membranes (upper section of Figure  4a and Figure S2, Supporting Information). The lower section of Figure  4a shows the energy barrier in subsequent sections of the channel as a result of the combined use of poly(sodium-4-styrenesulfonate) (PSS) molecules inside the passable cavity of $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and the energy surge wall barrier from the upper section of Figure  4a, collectively named energy surge baffle. The results show that the added presence of PSS magnifies the maximum energy barrier, which happens abruptly at the transitional zone.
Besides the size sieving, selectivity mainly depends on two factors: the trapping energy and the hydration energy. The trapping energy is the tendency of the passing cations to be absorbed by the intercalated nucleophile molecules inside the channel. Similarly, hydration energy is defined by the tendency of the water molecules to fully hydrate the cation. In order to reach a high selectivity, unwanted cations should be absorbed by the nucleophile molecules (PSS), which requires dehydration. When ions pass through the connecting section between the two MOF layers, the number of water molecules is further reduced (Figure S28, Supporting Information), forcing cations into a highly susceptible state in the presence of - ${\cdot\mathrm{SO}_{3}}$ and native - $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ in the UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ segment.
If the trapping energy overcomes the hydration energy, the cation will likely be stopped near the sulfonate groups (Figure 4b). While both energies are close, the trapping energy is generally higher than the hydration energy. The difference between the two energies is not large enough for all cations to be trapped in the sulfonate functional groups’ vicinity. Yet, its maximization in ${\mathrm{Li^{+}}}$ makes ${\cdot}{\mathrm{SO}}_{3}$ a suitable trap for catching Li cations (Figure $4\mathrm{b}$ , Figures S2 and S29, Supporting Information). Reaching as high as two, the trapping/hydration energy ratio shows the remarkable effectiveness of the trap for catching $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ cations. This ratio for the other cations is near unity, allowing them to pass through the channel with a small amount trapped within the pores. In the absence of PSS molecules, the - $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups intercalated within the $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ channel can limit the cations’ drift, especially $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ , whilst they find little success in trapping them (Figure S30, Supporting Information).
The energy surge baffle increases the adsorption preference of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ ions, as evident in the experimental permselectivity diagrams in Figure $\measuredangle4\boldsymbol{\mathrm{c}}$ (complete list is brought in Table S5, Supporting Information). Comparing the $\mathrm{K^{+}/L i^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaNa^{+}/L i^{+}}$ selectivity values for several filter designs shows that using two fused sections (energy surge wall mechanism) provides a higher selectivity ratio compared to other conventional channels. However, intercalating chemical species in order to create the energy surge baffle mechanism significantly increases the already enhanced selectivity to nearly ten times its initial values. The $\mathrm{K^{+}/L i^{+}}$ or $\mathrm{\DeltaNa^{+}/L i^{+}}$ transport ratio of the asymmetric U $\mathrm{iO{-}66{\cdot}N H_{2}(P S S)/Z I F{\cdot}8(P V P)}$ membrane is two orders of magnitude higher than previously developed membranes. This high selectivity ratio stems from the high asymmetrical structure of the membrane’s nanopores and the asymmetrical distribution of PSS molecules inside the nanochannels. The membranes developed here can be placed perpendicular to the direction of ionic flow which maximizes its ion conductivity and makes it suitable for potential industrial applications. The high surface area and low thickness of the membrane have resulted in its high ion conductance $0.24\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ for K+ions). The lithium enrichment ratio and $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ enrichment rate of the membrane are compared to other membranes in Figure S26, Supporting Information. Our membrane has a higher transport of $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ than $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ which means that when used in ion separation applications, it creates a lithium-rich feed by transporting $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ ions to the permeate side and keeping the ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ ions in the feed side. Other membranes that have a higher transport rate of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ to $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ work differently. Therefore, in order to be able to compare the MOF-on-MOF membranes to other membranes, lithium enrichment ratio was defined as $\mathrm{K^{+}/L i^{+}}$ or $\mathrm{Li^{+}/K^{+}}$ transport ratio (whichever is bigger) and the lithium enrichment rate was defined as the ion flux of the faster ion. As can be seen, the Ui ${\mathsf{O}}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}(\mathrm{PSS})/{\mathrm{ZIF}}{-}8(\mathrm{PVP})$ membrane developed in this study, even though it has a much higher ${\mathrm{Li^{+}}}$ enrichment ratio than other membranes, has not sacrificed its ion permeation and has a high ion flux as well.
In the end, we did an analysis on how the UiO-66- $\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}}(\ensuremath{\mathrm{PSS}})/$ ZIF-8(PVP) membrane can be potentially used to extract $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ ions from a brine containing a very high amount of $\mathrm{Na^{+}}$ (such as geothermal brines). It is shown that in the case of a brine with $30\ 000\ \mathrm{ppm\Na^{+}}$ and $200\ \mathrm{ppm\Li^{+}}$ , $98.7\%$ of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ ions can be recovered with this membrane in two cycles assuming a successful scale-up. One square meter of the membrane can recover all the ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ ions in $1000\mathrm{~L~}$ of this brine under $65\mathrm{~h~}$ in just two cycles. This achievement is only possible because of the membrane’s high selectivity ratio and its high ICR (Figure S31, Supporting Information).
In summary, we discovered a distinctive asymmetric channel architecture manufactured by consecutive synthesis of two different MOFs both intercalated with different polymers, which can provide significant $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ selectivity and rectification. The fundamental mechanism employed here, the energy surge wall and baffle, is based on the synergy between the nucleophile guest molecules and the sudden change to the size of the accessible cavity at the interface of the two MOF layers. Among manufactured prototypes, UiO $\lvert-66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}(\mathrm{PSS})/\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8(\mathrm{PVP})$ showed the highest cross-selectivity ratios of $\mathrm{Na^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}$ , and $\mathrm{Ca^{2+}}$ to ${\mathrm{Li^{+}}}$ of 80, 84, 29, and 52, respectively. Here, the sulfonate functional groups in PSS also provide a nucleophilic entrance that significantly increases the flow capacity and the directivity. The new architecture that we developed widely benefits the research on selective membranes by introducing a simple method for producing asymmetrical $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ -selective nanochannel based on consecutive synthesis and targeted intercalation of two conventional MOFs. Further study can also be conducted on glassy MOFs in the future, where the absence of grain boundaries may allow for the ultrafast transport of ions through the MOF glass.
